## Product Mental Model

Figma Make should understand UpVisit as a platform with two connected sides:

### 1. CMS / Admin side

Used by organisers, partners, exhibitors, sponsors, and internal teams to create, manage, publish, and analyse event experiences.

Core concepts:

- Event setup
- Experience setup
- Content management
- Exhibitor and sponsor profiles
- Speaker profiles
- Agenda
- Map and navigation
- Push notifications
- Surveys
- Matchmaking setup
- Analytics
- Integrations
- White-label configuration

### 2. Visitor app side

Used by participants before, during, and after the event.

Core concepts:

- Event guide
- Interactive map
- Navigation
- Personal agenda
- Exhibitor and sponsor discovery
- Speaker and session discovery
- Matchmaking
- Lead/contact exchange
- Push notifications
- Surveys
- Gamification
- Saved items
- QR codes
- Engagement with content and people

The platform value comes from connecting these two sides: organisers create a structured digital experience, and visitors generate engagement data, leads, interactions, and measurable value.

---

## Main Product Areas and Capabilities

### Self-Service CMS Platform

The CMS is the control centre for creating, managing, and publishing events and digital experiences.

Capabilities:

- Create an event or experience
- Publish app content
- Manage event information
- Manage agenda
- Manage exhibitors
- Manage sponsors
- Manage speakers
- Manage maps
- Manage push notifications
- Manage surveys
- Review analytics
- Configure integrations
- Support white-label setups

Design guidance:

- The CMS should feel fast, guided, and self-service.
- Avoid making setup feel like a long IT project.
- Event organisers should be able to launch useful experiences quickly.

---

### Event Apps

UpVisit creates individual event apps or app experiences for visitors, sponsors, speakers, and exhibitors.

Capabilities:

- Mobile event guide
- Event overview
- Personal programme
- Interactive map
- Exhibitor and sponsor discovery
- Speaker and session browsing
- Push notifications
- Matchmaking
- Surveys
- Gamification
- Contact and lead features

Design guidance:

- App experiences should prioritise event-day usefulness.
- Visitors need fast access to what is happening now, where to go, who to meet, and what to save.

---

### AI-Supported Content Creation

UpVisit uses AI support to help organisers create content faster.

Use cases:

- Generate event descriptions
- Improve writing
- Shorten text
- Lengthen text
- Change tone
- Change point of view
- Create app content from limited input
- Speed up event setup

Design guidance:

- AI should reduce setup effort.
- Always keep organiser control visible.
- Do not present AI as replacing the event organiser’s judgement.

---

### Interactive 3D Map and Navigation

Interactive maps are a core differentiator.

Capabilities:

- 3D venue or floor maps
- Indoor and outdoor navigation
- Booth or location markers
- Guided routes
- Themed paths
- Tour navigation
- Points of interest
- Spatial orientation

Design guidance:

- Map features should solve on-site complexity.
- Visitors should be able to find exhibitors, stages, rooms, amenities, routes, and points of interest quickly.
- Do not reduce the map to a static floor plan.

---

### Agenda and Personalised Event Programme

Visitors can use the app to understand what is happening and build their own programme.

Capabilities:

- Event programme
- Sessions
- Stages
- Time slots
- Speaker links
- Personal calendar
- Live “currently running” information
- Saved sessions

Design guidance:

- The agenda should support both discovery and planning.
- Personal agenda features should connect to reminders, navigation, and live context.

---

### Exhibitor, Sponsor, and Speaker Profiles

UpVisit gives stakeholders digital presence inside the event experience.

Profile types:

- Exhibitor profile
- Sponsor profile
- Speaker profile
- Partner profile

Possible profile content:

- Description
- Logo
- Images
- Videos
- Links
- Offers
- Contact options
- LinkedIn connection
- Booth location
- Sessions
- Lead capture actions

Design guidance:

- Profiles should support discovery, engagement, and conversion.
- Exhibitor and sponsor visibility is part of monetisation.

---

### Matchmaking and Networking

UpVisit supports intelligent matchmaking between exhibitors and participants.

Capabilities:

- Visitor profiles
- Exhibitor matching
- Participant recommendations
- Lead generation
- Direct contact
- Networking before, during, and after the event
- LinkedIn-supported connection flows

Design guidance:

- Matchmaking should feel relevant and consent-aware.
- Do not assume all visitors want to be identifiable by default.
- Participants may remain anonymous unless they voluntarily release data or create a profile.

---

### Lead Capture and LinkedIn Integration

UpVisit uses LinkedIn integration to support lead capture and contact import.

Capabilities:

- Lead generation for exhibitors
- Contact exchange
- LinkedIn profile connection
- Importing leads to LinkedIn or connected systems
- Visitor-to-exhibitor conversion

Design guidance:

- Lead features must preserve user control and consent.
- The value is not just collecting contacts, but connecting qualified event interest to exhibitors and sponsors.

---

### Push Notifications

Push notifications help organisers reach visitors before, during, and after the event.

Use cases:

- Agenda reminders
- Live updates
- Important announcements
- Sponsor messages
- Location-based prompts
- Event changes
- Real-time engagement

Design guidance:

- Notifications should be timely and targeted.
- Avoid generic spam-like notification flows.
- Consider frequency, relevance, consent, and event context.

---

### Surveys and Feedback

UpVisit supports participant surveys.

Capabilities:

- Create visitor surveys
- Collect feedback
- Measure satisfaction
- Capture event insights
- Link survey data to event performance

Design guidance:

- Surveys should be quick to create and easy for visitors to complete.
- Feedback should feed into analytics and future event optimisation.

---

### Gamification and Challenges

Gamification can increase engagement during the event.

Capabilities:

- Challenges
- QR scans
- Participation incentives
- Visitor journeys
- Sponsored interactions
- Unlockable content or steps

Design guidance:

- Gamification should support event goals, not distract from them.
- It should encourage movement, discovery, networking, and sponsor/exhibitor engagement.

---

### Live Analytics and Engagement Data

Analytics provide insight into event performance.

Possible analytics:

- Active users
- App impressions
- Content views
- Sponsor engagement
- Exhibitor engagement
- Map interactions
- Survey completion
- Matchmaking activity
- Lead generation
- Push notification performance
- Visitor behaviour
- Real-time event performance

Design guidance:

- Analytics should be useful to organisers, exhibitors, and sponsors.
- Reporting should show what worked, where, when, and with which audience.
- Avoid generic dashboards that do not connect to event success.

---

### Integrations and APIs

UpVisit can connect with existing systems.

Integration types:

- LinkedIn
- CRM systems such as HubSpot
- Ticketing systems
- App connections to ticket or CRM systems
- White-label API structures
- Custom integrations

Design guidance:

- Integration direction should be clear.
- Do not assume UpVisit replaces ticketing. It often complements ticketing as an engagement layer.
- Ticketing systems handle transactions; UpVisit unlocks engagement, insights, and revenue beyond the ticket.

---

### White-Label and API Platform

UpVisit can be used as a white-label platform for partners, agencies, system integrators, or existing event-tech providers.

Capabilities:

- Own branding
- Domain control
- User control
- Modular architecture
- APIs
- Admin
- Analytics
- Ready-to-customise platform

Design guidance:

- White-label flows should make brand ownership, client context, and platform boundaries clear.
- Avoid designing everything as UpVisit-branded if the feature is for white-label use.

---

## Important Product Objects and Concepts

### Event

A specific B2B event, fair, conference, summit, exhibition, cultural event, city event, or tourism experience.

### Experience

The digital app-based representation of an event, place, tour, or programme.

### Event organiser

The organisation or person responsible for setting up and managing the event experience.

### Visitor / participant

A person attending or using the app experience.

### Exhibitor

A company or organisation presenting itself at the event.

### Sponsor

A commercial stakeholder paying for visibility, promotion, or engagement.

### Speaker

A person presenting in the event programme.

### CMS

The platform area where organisers create, manage, and publish the digital experience.

### Event app

The visitor-facing mobile experience.

### App guide

The app-based guide that helps visitors navigate and engage with the event.

### Interactive map

A map that helps visitors understand and navigate the venue or place.

### 3D map

A spatial map used for immersive navigation and orientation.

### Guided tour / themed path

A route through locations, content, or points of interest.

### Agenda

The structured event programme.

### Personal programme

The visitor’s selected or saved schedule.

### Matchmaking

Logic that connects participants and exhibitors or other participants based on interests, profiles, or event context.

### Lead

A visitor or participant who voluntarily shares contact information or shows relevant interest.

### LinkedIn integration

A connection used for lead capture, profile connection, or contact import.

### Push notification

A real-time message sent to visitors before, during, or after the event.

### Challenge

A gamified task or interaction within the event app.

### Survey

A feedback form used to collect participant input.

### Analytics

Data about visitor engagement, content performance, sponsor/exhibitor results, and event performance.

### Engagement data

Behavioural data generated by app use, interactions, content views, navigation, surveys, matchmaking, and lead actions.

### White-label

A deployment where the platform or app experience appears under another brand.

### API integration

A technical connection to other systems such as CRM, ticketing, or partner platforms.

